WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.765
Software agents are starting to act for us.

2
00:00:02.765 --> 00:00:06.639
They compare mortgages, share our data, and agree things in our name.

3
00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:14.613
This is Answerable: a design for an agent that can be held to account, the specifications behind it, and a working agent you can step through.

4
00:00:15.462 --> 00:00:17.506
The starting point is uncomfortable.

5
00:00:17.506 --> 00:00:21.822
Today's models can be talked into things, and no filter reliably stops that.

6
00:00:21.822 --> 00:00:24.945
So the checks that matter cannot live inside the model.

7
00:00:25.795 --> 00:00:26.957
Here is the design.

8
00:00:26.957 --> 00:00:30.626
The agent acts for a principal: a person or an organization.

9
00:00:30.626 --> 00:00:33.744
In the middle is the core, the model and its tools.

10
00:00:33.744 --> 00:00:35.395
We treat it as a black box.

11
00:00:35.395 --> 00:00:38.208
It holds no keys, and it cannot send anything.

12
00:00:38.208 --> 00:00:39.431
It can only propose.

13
00:00:40.281 --> 00:00:41.586
Around it sit two gates.

14
00:00:41.586 --> 00:00:43.542
They are ordinary code, not a model.

15
00:00:43.542 --> 00:00:47.563
The inbound gate checks everything that comes in, before the core sees it.

16
00:00:47.563 --> 00:00:51.801
The outbound gate checks everything the core proposes, before anything leaves.

17
00:00:52.652 --> 00:00:58.085
Underneath are four stores: the policies, the memory, a trust store, and a record of every decision.

18
00:00:58.936 --> 00:01:01.986
What travels between agents is a verifiable message.

19
00:01:01.986 --> 00:01:05.094
It might go by web call, by email, or through a tool.

20
00:01:05.094 --> 00:01:08.026
However it travels, the carriage is never trusted.

21
00:01:08.877 --> 00:01:10.428
Inside is a signed bundle.

22
00:01:10.428 --> 00:01:11.144
Who sent it.

23
00:01:11.144 --> 00:01:12.516
Their authority to act.

24
00:01:12.516 --> 00:01:13.232
The content.

25
00:01:13.232 --> 00:01:14.604
The evidence behind it.

26
00:01:14.604 --> 00:01:15.976
The terms for using it.

27
00:01:15.976 --> 00:01:18.542
And where it came from, and how good it is.

28
00:01:19.392 --> 00:01:23.708
No single standard covers all of that, so the specification is a family.

29
00:01:23.708 --> 00:01:25.806
Eleven layers, each one a question.

30
00:01:25.806 --> 00:01:27.545
How does a message get there?

31
00:01:27.545 --> 00:01:29.583
Who gave this agent its authority?

32
00:01:29.583 --> 00:01:31.382
How far can this be relied on?

33
00:01:32.232 --> 00:01:34.448
Each layer has interchangeable suites.

34
00:01:34.448 --> 00:01:36.898
One suite per layer makes a configuration.

35
00:01:36.898 --> 00:01:44.248
The filled ones are the baseline: the configuration every agent is asked to support, so that any two have something in common.

36
00:01:45.099 --> 00:01:46.366
This is the explorer.

37
00:01:46.366 --> 00:01:49.385
It replays a recorded run, one decision at a time.

38
00:01:49.385 --> 00:01:51.136
On the left is what Sam sees.

39
00:01:51.136 --> 00:01:53.128
In the middle are the two agents.

40
00:01:53.128 --> 00:01:55.966
On the right is the detail of the current step.

41
00:01:56.814 --> 00:01:59.494
The badge at the top says what you are watching.

42
00:01:59.494 --> 00:02:01.504
This one is a real run of the agent.

43
00:02:01.504 --> 00:02:04.128
Sam asks for a mortgage in principle on a flat.

44
00:02:04.128 --> 00:02:09.432
Sam has already signed a mandate, which says what the agent may do, and when it must ask first.

45
00:02:10.283 --> 00:02:14.525
Before anything goes to North Bank, the outbound gate asks who North Bank is.

46
00:02:14.525 --> 00:02:16.398
Sam's own short list has no entry.

47
00:02:16.398 --> 00:02:23.120
So the gate asks a national trust root, which points to the sector register, which says North Bank is authorized for this.

48
00:02:23.120 --> 00:02:27.968
It works like looking up a name on the internet, and the path is kept with the decision.

49
00:02:28.818 --> 00:02:33.163
The bank asks for evidence, and the core proposes sharing two statements.

50
00:02:33.163 --> 00:02:36.555
The outbound gate takes the proposal one check at a time.

51
00:02:36.555 --> 00:02:37.864
The mandate covers it.

52
00:02:37.864 --> 00:02:39.233
The recipient is known.

53
00:02:39.233 --> 00:02:41.078
The terms go out with the data.

54
00:02:41.078 --> 00:02:44.648
Then limits and approval: the mandate says to ask Sam first.

55
00:02:45.499 --> 00:02:47.119
So the agent stops and asks.

56
00:02:47.119 --> 00:02:52.442
The question Sam sees is written by the gate, from the rule and the facts, not by the model.

57
00:02:52.442 --> 00:02:56.261
Sam approves, the check passes, and only then does the message go.

58
00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:59.150
The real agent runs that core today.

59
00:02:59.150 --> 00:03:04.419
The full journey has more parties in it, and this next part is still a mock-up of the design.

60
00:03:04.419 --> 00:03:05.496
The banner says so.

61
00:03:06.346 --> 00:03:08.466
First the agent looks for products.

62
00:03:08.466 --> 00:03:12.343
It asks a public index, and even here the disclosure check runs.

63
00:03:12.343 --> 00:03:15.130
The question carries a loan band and a region.

64
00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:16.220
Nothing about Sam.

65
00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:20.907
To show the deposit, the agent asks Sam's own bank for a statement.

66
00:03:20.907 --> 00:03:22.339
Now watch the other side.

67
00:03:22.339 --> 00:03:27.551
The bank's inbound gate checks the mandate, and finds it was approved with a password only.

68
00:03:27.551 --> 00:03:29.555
For a balance, the bank wants more.

69
00:03:29.555 --> 00:03:30.987
Nothing reaches its core.

70
00:03:30.987 --> 00:03:32.247
A challenge goes back.

71
00:03:33.097 --> 00:03:35.989
Sam does a face check with an identity provider.

72
00:03:35.989 --> 00:03:39.784
The agent asks again, and this time the authority check passes.

73
00:03:40.635 --> 00:03:44.795
The statement comes back signed, and Sam's inbound gate takes its turn.

74
00:03:44.795 --> 00:03:45.557
Is it intact?

75
00:03:45.557 --> 00:03:48.136
Is this bank the right source for a balance?

76
00:03:48.136 --> 00:03:49.542
What terms came with it?

77
00:03:49.542 --> 00:03:55.812
Those terms stay attached in memory: mortgage assessment only, no onward sharing, delete after ninety days.

78
00:03:56.662 --> 00:04:00.354
The last inbound check is fitness: how far to rely on something.

79
00:04:00.354 --> 00:04:03.354
Memory already holds Sam's own note of their income.

80
00:04:03.354 --> 00:04:06.008
The payroll provider gives a different figure.

81
00:04:06.008 --> 00:04:09.412
Payroll outranks a self-report, so the payroll figure wins.

82
00:04:09.412 --> 00:04:11.604
The note is kept, marked as outranked.

83
00:04:12.454 --> 00:04:13.620
Now the application.

84
00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:17.290
The core prepares it, and the outbound gate goes down its list.

85
00:04:17.290 --> 00:04:20.728
Disclosure: the income figure and the balance summary only.

86
00:04:20.728 --> 00:04:25.855
Data terms: both statements allow this use, and their terms travel with the application.

87
00:04:26.704 --> 00:04:30.297
And here is the message itself, in the layers you saw earlier.

88
00:04:30.297 --> 00:04:31.746
Every part can be opened.

89
00:04:32.596 --> 00:04:35.263
Because the layers are separate, you can swap one.

90
00:04:35.263 --> 00:04:40.012
Change the transport from a web call to email, and the same checks run in the same order.

91
00:04:40.012 --> 00:04:41.399
Only the carriage changes.

92
00:04:42.248 --> 00:04:44.995
The design matters most when something goes wrong.

93
00:04:44.995 --> 00:04:48.071
In this real run, we make the core misbehave on purpose.

94
00:04:48.071 --> 00:04:53.126
After a message from the lender, it proposes paying a valuer four hundred and fifty dollars.

95
00:04:53.126 --> 00:04:57.411
The outbound gate finds nothing in Sam's mandate that allows it, and stops it.

96
00:04:57.411 --> 00:04:58.235
Nothing leaves.

97
00:04:59.084 --> 00:05:02.336
And here the lender asks for more than the mandate covers.

98
00:05:02.336 --> 00:05:03.009
Same result.

99
00:05:03.009 --> 00:05:05.980
The gate stops it, and the agent tells the lender no.

100
00:05:06.831 --> 00:05:09.117
Every check traces back to a requirement.

101
00:05:09.117 --> 00:05:13.858
This view takes the OWASP list of risks for agents, and shows the answer to each one.

102
00:05:14.707 --> 00:05:16.485
The specifications are here too.

103
00:05:16.485 --> 00:05:22.152
Eighteen documents, every requirement numbered, so a step in a run can point to the clause it applied.

104
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:27.355
And the roadmap shows what is done, what is being built, and what comes next.

105
00:05:27.355 --> 00:05:30.918
One thing to be plain about: both sides here run the same code.

106
00:05:30.918 --> 00:05:34.990
Showing that independent implementations work together is still to come.

107
00:05:35.841 --> 00:05:38.895
Most people will not adopt a new protocol in one step.

108
00:05:38.895 --> 00:05:40.196
They can receive email.

109
00:05:40.196 --> 00:05:46.812
So there is an entry configuration that works over ordinary email, with a short footer that says what the message is.

110
00:05:47.663 --> 00:05:49.665
Someone with no agent just replies.

111
00:05:49.665 --> 00:05:54.925
Someone with an agent can check it, and the two move up to the baseline one layer at a time.

112
00:05:55.776 --> 00:06:00.730
That is Answerable: the reference design, the specifications, and an agent that runs them.

113
00:06:00.730 --> 00:06:02.106
Every view can be linked.

114
00:06:02.106 --> 00:06:03.482
Step through it yourself.
